extraction_notes: "WB20 Canada printed 155-157 (cache p156-p158, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer with the top of printed 156 checked against a render. The heading Loup Garou and its lore are in the second column of printed 155; the stat block headed Loup Garou, NPC Monster & Villain, fills printed 156 and ends in the first column of printed 157, where Ogopogo begins. The book does not suggest it as a player character; it is imported as playable on the repo owner''s decision for this book''s optional creatures. Not tagged evil: the alignment line reads theoretically any. || EXPERIENCE - THE BOOK NAMES TWO TABLES: the Player Character Experience line says to use the same experience table as the Dragon, and the R.C.C. Skills & Instincts paragraph says to use the same experience table as the Psi-Stalker. The Notes under the Experience Tables on printed 192 say the Loup-garou uses the Dragon E.P. tables, so the Dragon ladder is stored, copied from the stored xp_table of dragon-hatchling (Dragon Hatchling (Great Horned), Rifts Ultimate Edition). The Psi-Stalker sentence is recorded here and not used. || HIT POINTS - PRINTED TWICE: a line Hit Points: 3D4x10 +P.E. number (text layer 3D4xlO, confirmed on the render) sits with P.P.E., life span and the NPC Experience Level line; a second line Hit Points (Special): 6D6+36 in addition to 2D6 H.P. per level of experience, see Natural Invulnerability, follows the Player Character Experience and Horror Factor lines. Stored: 6D6+36 plus 2D6 per level. It is the only one of the two that grows by level, and the skills paragraph a player character levels under says Hit Points and skill proficiency increase with each level; it is also the Hit Points (Special) form the other werebeast entries use. The 3D4x10 +P.E. figure is recorded here and in GM Notes and is not stored. No S.D.C. or M.D.C. is printed; men_of_arms false, the race rule. || P.P.E. 2D4x10+10 (text layer 2D4xlO+10, confirmed on the render). || ATTRIBUTES: P.S. 4D6+6 never less than 18 and P.P. 4D6 never less than 16 are attribute_minimums. Spd is 5D6 in human form (stored) or 1D6+48 as a wolf (natural ability text). || ONE SHAPE IS STORED, the werejaguar precedent: the class''s own block is the natural man-wolf shape, so attacks_base is 6 and horror_factor 12. The human and wolf shapes have four attacks, and the human shape has no Horror Factor; both are natural ability text. || BONUSES: +2 initiative, +3 strike, parry and dodge, +3 pull punch, +2 roll, +7 vs Horror Factor, +2 vs psionics, +2 vs magic stored as spell_magic. || PSIONICS: I.S.P. 5D6 +1D6 per level; Sense Evil, Sense Magic, Sixth Sense, See the Invisible and Mind Block granted by name; no tier is printed and none is written. || SKILLS: Gobblely at 98; Old Canadian French at 98 as Language: Other (no French row in the catalog); American or Euro at 98 as a pick between Language: Other and Language: Euro. Land navigation +20 (36+20), track animals +20 as Track & Trap Animals (20+20), track humanoids +25 as Tracking (people) (25+25). Prowl 80 and swim 70 are printed as natural abilities and granted as catalog skills at a fixed figure; track by smell 70% stays a natural ability. The book calls the rest Secondary Skills: seven at level one and two more at 4, 8, 12 and 15, stored as secondary_skills with the printed categories. Pilot boat is the three small-boat rows and horsemanship is Horsemanship: General (the werejaguar readings). The printed Rogue +5% and Wilderness +10% are in the note, to add by hand. || NOT STORED: size (human form 5-6.5 feet; wolf 4-5 feet at the shoulder and 7-9 feet long; man-wolf seven feet), weight 200-400 lb, life span 300 years, NPC level 1D6, habitat, enemies and allies; they are GM Notes. The line R.C.C.: Werewolf, Supernatural predator is a label. No equipment or money is printed."
---

## Lore

Loup Garou is the French-Canadian name for the werewolf, and the old
stories mean something worse by it than the ordinary kind. Every werebeast
is a supernatural predator that lives to hunt; the Loup Garou are said to
be closer to demons than to animals. They will eat game, but they prefer
humans and D-Bees, and they kill pets and livestock simply to hurt the
people who own them.

In the wolf shapes they feel untouchable, and that is how they challenge
rivals and take revenge. In human shape they work from the shadows as
schemers, bullies and backstabbers, driven by envy, greed, lust and rage.
Either way they take what they want and hoard it: gems, weapons, magic,
books, technology and keepsakes of those they have killed, all as proof of
their own superiority.

Most people thought them gone from North America long before the Rifts.
Several family clans survive in Quebec and Newfoundland: a few rule
wilderness towns, one deals arms, another is said to spy for Free Quebec,
and the rest are bandits, mercenaries or wild things running with wolf
packs and feuding with the Windigo. A clan numbers from a handful to a
couple of dozen. Their jealousy of one another keeps the total under four
hundred. A player''s Loup Garou is an outcast from all of this, and its
kin want it dead.

## GM Notes

The book heads the entry an NPC monster and villain and does not suggest
it for players. A player''s Loup Garou starts at level one.

Hit Points are printed twice. The sheet uses the Special line, 6D6+36 plus
2D6 per level. The other line reads 3D4x10 plus the P.E. number, with no
growth by level.

Experience is named twice as well: the Dragon table in the experience line
and the Psi-Stalker table in the skills paragraph. The note under the
book''s Experience Tables says Dragon, and that ladder is used.

The sheet shows the man-wolf shape: six attacks and Horror Factor 12. In
human or wolf form it has four attacks; in human form it has no Horror
Factor and may wear M.D.C. armor. As a wolf its Spd is 1D6+48.

Secondary skills: Rogue picks are +5% and Wilderness picks +10%; add them
by hand.

Size: 5-6.5 feet (1.5 to 1.9 m) in human form; as a wolf 4-5 feet (1.2 to
1.5 m) at the shoulder and 7-9 feet (2.1 to 2.7 m) long; seven feet
(2.1 m) as the man-wolf. Weight 200-400 pounds (90 to 180 kg). Average
life span 300 years. NPCs average 1D6 levels.

Clans: the smallest 1D6+2 members, the average 2D6+4, the largest 3D6+8.
Rumor puts another 50-100 in the Louisiana bayou, exiles from Canada
centuries ago with no love for their northern cousins, and a large pack
led by Loup Garou in the mountains of Romania.

Habitat: Eastern Canada and Louisiana only; the ordinary Werewolf is more
widespread. Enemies: supernatural predators, humans and humanoids; the
Windigo, D''Sonoqua and Russian Demons are rivals to destroy. Allies: may
side with other supernatural or evil forces, and Shifters sometimes
summon them as assassins.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'loup-garou');


